WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.390 --> 00:00:09.817
My name is Max Tarmossin,

00:00:09.817 --> 00:00:13.170
and I'm the owner and
operator of ColorGlo GTA.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:17.007
Our main job is to refresh and restore

00:00:17.007 --> 00:00:18.864
interior components of vehicles.

00:00:18.864 --> 00:00:20.405
Today, I'll be walking you through

00:00:20.405 --> 00:00:22.521
how I'm restoring the driver's seat

00:00:22.521 --> 00:00:26.270
on the 2006 Ferrari 360 Spider.

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:28.143
The problem with the driver's seat

00:00:28.143 --> 00:00:30.530
is that over the years of use,

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:33.280
there's obvious signs of wear and tear.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:36.610
The original dye has
faded, it started cracking.

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:39.850
The leather has started
to wrinkle and fold up

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:41.543
and deform itself.

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:49.010
The first thing that I do with this car

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:52.930
is I start by removing the
seat from the vehicle itself.

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:54.670
That makes it much easier to work on,

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:57.080
and when I end up spraying the dye,

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:59.993
I don't have to mask
off the entire interior.

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:02.590
Once the seat is out of the car,

00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:04.260
I put it on my worktable

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:08.340
and I start by dismantling
the seat itself.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:11.540
I'm removing the main bottom
cushion and the top cushion

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:14.430
to expose the bolsters of the seat.

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:16.310
This makes my life a lot easier,

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:19.310
because I gain access to
all the little crevices

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:20.660
that are found in the seat.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:28.830
I start by fully cleaning the seat.

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:30.930
I have my own cleaning mixture.

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:33.720
I spray the seat down, and
I start off with a brush,

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:35.590
and I start agitating the seat

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:37.645
to lift the dirt up from the leather

00:01:37.645 --> 00:01:40.070
that's been in there for years.

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:41.550
This is a very crucial step,

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:43.823
because I want a clean working slate.

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:46.260
The reason for that is

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:49.280
when I'll be applying
the fillers and the dyes,

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:50.913
it has something to stick on

00:01:50.913 --> 00:01:53.204
and there's no grease or oils

00:01:53.204 --> 00:01:55.543
that could alter or affect my products.

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:00.350
I use a soft horsehair brush

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:02.240
to get into all the little crevices

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:04.440
and to clean up the threads,

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:07.330
and then I follow up by
drying it with a hair dryer.

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:09.450
I could leave it to dry by itself,

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:11.730
but I find that it's much quicker

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:13.760
and I can see any parts that I missed

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:16.983
because they still remain
shiny and not matte.

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:27.343
Once the cleaning has been fully done,

00:02:27.343 --> 00:02:30.120
then I start by sanding the leather down.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:33.140
It creates trenches in the leather

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:37.230
that enables my product
to bond with the leather

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:38.908
to have proper adhesion.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:43.730
I'm removing the old dye
that's all cracked up,

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:48.100
and I'm exposing the natural
hide on the seat itself.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:50.730
I usually start with 120 grit,

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:54.130
and I work my way up to 400 grit.

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:55.830
The smooth finish is needed

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:59.079
because once I start applying the dye,

00:02:59.079 --> 00:03:00.422
you don't want the leather to be

00:03:00.422 --> 00:03:03.110
still uneven when the dye lays on it,

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:05.623
and looks natural, clean, and uniform.

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:10.427
Then I start filling in any imperfections,

00:03:10.427 --> 00:03:13.260
scratches with our crack filler.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:16.530
Our fillers are flexible
because leather is flexible

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:19.960
and we can't be using a stiff filler.

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:21.650
I use an artist's spatula.

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:25.030
It allows me to fill the cracks

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:29.370
but also remove the excess
filler from the top surface.

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:32.370
I carefully go around the entire seat.

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:35.210
I let that sit to dry for a bit,

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:38.570
and, once again, I go back to sanding.

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:41.473
It creates a smooth, uniform finish.

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:50.520
I start by mixing the color.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:52.760
In this case, the color is black,

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:54.640
so it's fairly simple.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:57.640
I'm pouring my base into the green cup.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:02.405
Then I pour the black pigment.

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:12.548
Once I've established the ratio

00:04:12.548 --> 00:04:15.920
for how consistent I need the dye to be,

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:17.810
I move on to the next step,

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:21.051
and that is starting to hand-rub

00:04:21.051 --> 00:04:23.210
the dye into the leather.

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:26.010
It allows the dye to be sucked

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:28.080
into those trenches and valleys,

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:31.950
and it allows the leather
to really absorb our dye

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:34.250
and to hang onto it.

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:37.230
I go over the entire seat.

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:41.487
Once I've done that, I pour
the dye into my spray gun

00:04:41.487 --> 00:04:44.300
and I add additives to my dye.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:47.180
So, in this case, the
additives that I've added

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:50.300
was a no-gloss additive.

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:53.133
Usually a full seat receives

00:04:53.133 --> 00:04:56.890
about three to four coats of dye.

00:04:56.890 --> 00:04:58.275
Once I've been spraying,

00:04:58.275 --> 00:05:01.525
the white crack filler
is no longer visible,

00:05:01.525 --> 00:05:03.170
and that's what I'm aiming at.

00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:05.180
I take out my second gun,

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:07.763
and now into that gun I mix in

00:05:07.763 --> 00:05:09.921
the same leather base that I started with,

00:05:09.921 --> 00:05:12.300
but without any black pigment,

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:15.600
and this serves as a
clear coat for the seat,

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:19.733
which separates the
black dye from the user.

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:27.450
Curing takes about a day,

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:30.142
then I go ahead and reinstall the seat.

